A||||16||Kwez||88 vs others||||1||
Z||000000||Kwez||06-15-2001||04:25 PM||||ok, <BR>since I can't afford an 88 for now & am not very patient ;-) ...<P>my (previous) latest plan was to get an 88, upgrade the dampers & coils, 16" wheels w. good rubber, maybe better pads for the brakes & steel braided lines ? & eventually an engine swap.<P>I really wanted a 5speed but they're very hard to find & just too expensive. (88)<P>I'm thinking again of an 86-87 w. upgraded suspension, brakes, wheels etc..<BR>(I don't have the money now, I'm actually selling my 86GT 3speed autom., but could afford a cheaper one that I would upgrade later ... <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011261.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011261.html</A>  )<P><BR>What would be the advantages for either solution, 88 vs others ?<BR>ride (confort) ?<BR>handling ?<BR>highway stability ?<BR>how good are the stock 88 brakes ? <P>keep in mind that the final goal is a 'pocket rocket', the engine swap is a sure thing in the future & I want the handling & 'braking' to be on par.<BR>(I'm aiming for 250-300HP, closer to 300)<P>I have read stuff like "I didn't see a difference between the 88 / 87" from car magazines ... but also read "I would never go back to a pre 88 .. you can keep your Citation suspension" from someone on this board, etc...<P>So, I know it all depends on how hot I want the car to be, how much I want to spend, but I would really like to hear people's opinion on this ...<P>so gentlemen (& ladies) start your ... typing !<P>thx||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fformula88||06-15-2001||04:52 PM||||The major difference is the 88's have a redesigned suspension.  As all the old reviews stated, it is an improvement over the pre-88's, but I wouldn't stay away from an earlier year car over this.  My 88 Formula, and my fathers 85 SE both have the WS6 suspension package, but of course mine is the new design.  Handling wise I do not notice much of a difference.  I think the 88's ride a little smoother over bumps.  However, the actual performance of the suspension on everyday driving doesn't seem very different.  <P>The stock 88 brakes are good, but not great.  The main advantage is they have vented rotors.  I have never been too impressed with any Fiero brakes, but again the difference isn't enough to make me choose an 88 over another year car in equal condition.<P>So as far as never going back to the old suspension, for me the difference doesn't feel big enough to put too much weight on it in a purchase decision.  There are many other important factors to consider (milage, frame rust, interior condition, paint etc etc)<BR><P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierosig2.JPG"> <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||06-15-2001||05:04 PM||||This is going to turn into a flame war!<P>My 88 formula takes bumps way better than my coupe ever did. My 87 coupe would slam over any bump bigger than an expansion joint. The ride is much more composed and forgiving in the 88 car. <P>They both handle about the same on perfect roads except my 88 feels more neutral at the limit than the previous years do and it’s easier to control. Throw in some bumps and the handling difference becomes very apparent right away. <P>Highway stability they are about the same. My 87 coupe actually felt a little more tossable because of the lighter weight. <BR> <BR> I have driven an 87 GT. The 88 brakes just feel better and there is less pedal effort to make the car stop. My 88 brakes feel much better than my 87 coupe brakes ever did even with the Baretta upgrade in the front and new pads, rotors, and calipers all around. <P>The body flexes less in the 88. The interior panels in my 87 coupe used to creek and squeak when I drove onto the ramp of my driveway.<P>The suspension and brakes are way better and the solid mounted rear cradle and beefy front cradle make the frame feel more rigid. <P>After owning an 88 I could never go back to an earlier year. I guess you don’t miss what you never had. I sold my coupe because I got to drive an 88. If I had not driven the 88formula I probably would not have a fiero right now. <P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hartz||06-15-2001||05:34 PM||||If you were going to leave your car stock, then yes, the '88's ride better, have a lot of nice refinements, etc.  But from your post above, it sounds like you're going to be doing some mods.  If that's the case, I'd say any differences will be neglibable or negated by the time you're done.<P>Best of luck,<BR>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||06-15-2001||07:34 PM||||I've got an '88 Formula, and an '86SE. Both are with v-6, and both are manual. The difference between the two is night and day. Today I started my Formula up after 11 days of driving my SE. I had a smile on my face before I even got out of the driveway. Everything just feels better. I love my '86, but it is a beast to drive. My '88s brakes are better, handles better, the steering is lighter, and everything seems to come together on my '88, where my '86 feels(and is) very loose. <P>The '88s have drawbacks, parts are hard to find and more expensive etc. <P>I was originally looking for a cheap little Fiero as I was still in High-school with very little money, I kept waiting until I found the right Fiero, and one day I found a Formula in my price range, and was exactly what I was looking for, but didn't think I could afford.<P>The Fiero is by no means a "super-car" or anything I really feel is worth bragging about in stock form, but it is a great setup to start upgrading.||||reg||1
Z||000005||sjp777||06-15-2001||09:06 PM||||The 88's handle way better than the others. They turn better, they ride better and they stop better. I've had both, it's a big difference. You would have to dump a lot of money into suspension and brake upgrades on an 84 to 87 to get it too handle like the stock 88.<P>In the long run, the initial higher price of the 88 outweighs the expense you would have getting the others to the same level.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_4.jpg"> <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tweek||06-15-2001||11:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88formula:<BR><B>My 87 coupe would slam over any bump bigger than an expansion joint</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What, may I ask, is an expansion joint?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Dave Gunsul||06-15-2001||11:33 PM||||This is going to come as a shock to everyone<BR>but I like the 84-87's better.<BR>The 88's do ride SMOOTHER but they really don't handle any better. (if you want a smoother ride get a caddy)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>However, the 88's seem a little more numb to me. One of my favorite things about the Fiero is that they have great feel for the road and the so called refinement's take some<BR>of that away. <BR>The 88's feel like they have power steering<BR>is what i'm really trying to say.<BR>The 88's brakes are better too but there's<BR>a lot more aftermarket suspension and brake<BR>conversions for the 84-87's so if you're going to change the car anyway, what's the point??<BR>If you're looking for a pocket-rocket the 85<BR>GT and SE are the lightest that come with a V6. 2572 Ib's with stick!<P>No flames please, we are all entitled to our opinions.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver<p>[This message has been edited by Dave Gunsul (edited 06-15-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000008||danny boy||06-15-2001||11:46 PM||||Dave, if you think the '88s feel like power steering, try takin the steering dampener off the front of a pre'88 car. Wow! <BR> I put a big ass rear swaybar and coilovers on my 87 GT and did the dampener thing.Front suspension is stock. Handles about like the stock '88 I got to drive. 88 brakes are way better,easier to use. One thing you should think of- pre-88 cars are much easier to find(suspension) parts for and are also cheaper once you find them. An'88 subframe will bolt to an early car chassis with an adapter kit from Dave Held.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||06-16-2001||10:54 AM||||I don't have any experience with the '88 rear, but the '88 front absolutely light years ahead of the '87 front. The better geometry of the '88 absolutely can not be beaten by an early suspension no matter how much it's modified.<P>I drove an '86 SE w/ transplanted '88 coupe front suspension for a while before I drove my '87 GT.<P>I've driven both to 110%, and I have to say the difference is night and day.<P>The average daily driver will only notice the reduced steering effort of an '88.<P>But when you push to car to its limit and beyond, the '88 front suspension really shines. It tells you <I>exactly</I> what's going on with the front contact patch.<P>The '87 front suspension hardly tells me anything about what the front contact patch is doing.<P>My '87 suspension is more tricked out than the '88 ever was. Right now I have delrin front bushings, aluminum cradle bushings, UHMW rear bushings, and a WS6 front bar on the rear with rod end sway bar links at all four corners.<P>The '88 suspension still had original bushings. The '86 SE had a WS6 front bar on the rear, but still had rubber cradle bushings and rubber control arm bushings.<P>Both cars had new ball joints.<P>Despite the difference in mods, the '86 with '88 suspension was still a better driver's car.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 06-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Will||06-16-2001||10:55 AM||||Hey, look at that!<BR>My first double post!<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 06-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Toddster||06-16-2001||12:22 PM||||Get an '88.<P>You can get an '88 coupe for very little money. I bought a one owner '88 coupe recently for $700. You can upgrade or swap the engine as you like. They came with 5-speed trannys (Isuzu, not Getrag/Muncie) and they have the same suspension as the Formula and GT (except for the rear antisway bar and stiffer springs). you can upgrade all of this in time. But at least you have the BASICs you need for a great car.<P>Take it from someone who owns a Pace Car and several '88s. Once you go '88 you never go back!||||reg||1
Z||000012||WampusCat||06-16-2001||01:53 PM||||There are deals on 88's out there.  Check this 45k mi GT out for $1900.  Too bad it's automatic trans.  <A HREF="http://www.autotrader.com/findacar/vdetail.jtmpl?car_id=67904515&dealer_id=&certified=n&max_price=3000&start_year=1988&end_year=1988&address=38104&search_type=used&make=PONT&m]" TARGET=_blank>http://www.autotrader.com/findacar/vdetail.jtmpl?car_id=67904515&dealer_id=&certified=n&max_price=3000&start_year=1988&end_year=1988&address=38104&search_type=used&make=PONT&m]</A> <p>[This message has been edited by WampusCat (edited 06-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Wannabe||06-16-2001||02:11 PM||||I have driven both '87 GT and '88 Formula on the track. Both with stock suspension, although the '87 had serious high performance tires, while the '88 only has s-rated all seasons. The difference is huge on the track. The '88 is VERY easy to push hard, and in spite of some very stupid driving my last time out, the car was extremely forgiving. The '87 was very hard to get a feel for and could not be pushed. I was driving as hard as I could and when I saw the lousy lap time I almost had a heart attack. ON the street, the difference is not as important, bus still definitely noticable. If you are the kind of person who likes to push your car from time to time, or think you might want to try out some track work, you will definitely have more fun with an '88. In my opinion of course  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||Kwez||06-16-2001||03:46 PM||||sounds like an 88 is the thing for me.<P>I like to know that the car is not going to go nuts if I hit a bump in a curve! I'm no Villeneuve... (same province (Quebec), different driving skills ;-)<P>I did talk to a guy from Mtl that autocrosses his Fiero & he mentionned that they (Fieros) are more difficult to drive fast (than other cars). <P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>ON the street, the difference is not as important, bus still definitely noticable. If you are the kind of person who likes to push your car from time to time, or think you might want to try out some track work, you will definitely have more fun with an '88<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I couldn't have described myself better   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Toddster- I never considered that option (88 coupe), it might be the best for me since I want to swap most of the stuff anyway ... specially considering my current financial resources.<BR>My only concern here is the Isuzu trans.<P><BR>Again, thank you very much, all of your comments were very helpfull.<P>Now it's time for me to learn to be patient   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Kwez (edited 06-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Raydar||06-17-2001||12:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Kwez:<BR><B>My only concern here is the Isuzu trans.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Don't sweat the Isuzu. Several folks on this Forum have installed it behind (beside?) the V6. Even 3.4s. BBBAD GT has an Isuzu in his V-8 84.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>Supposedly, the weakest part of the Isuzu is the shift fork(s). As long as you don't slam gears when you shift, it should be okay.<BR>Maybe someone else will jump in.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"||||reg||1
Z||000016||Kwez||06-17-2001||03:01 AM||||ok, thx.<BR>The reason I was worried is because of the planned engine swap .. far in the future but it will influence my 'base car' choice.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Kwez (edited 06-17-2001).]||||reg||7
